  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA mid/upper-level ridge over the area early this morning is \nflattened by a weak flat wave coming out of a long wave trough\nout west today, before rebuilding over the area tonight. The \nflat wave is associated with a cluster of showers and \nthunderstorms approaching from the midwest early this morning. \nThis activity continues to fall apart this morning, and then \nreforms just west of and over the area this afternoon, as is \nsuggested by most guidance including the Convective Allowing \nModels. Storms could then organize into a small cluster or two.\n\nCentral Guidance has been close or even a bit low on dew \npoints, which have been in the mid to even upper 70s at ASOS\nsites. With heating taking temperatures up to around 90 degrees\nthis afternoon, and the -10 C level just above h5, this \ngenerates CAPE up around 3500. Coupled with 30 kts mid-level \nflow north with the flat wave, thunderstorms, once formed, can \ntower quickly and produce heavy downpours, and strong to \ndamaging wind gusts including microbursts.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has a Slight Risk for Severe\nThunderstorms in and near the mountains, with a Marginal Risk \nback over the middle Ohio Valley and down along the Tug Fork, \nfor this afternoon and early this evening. This is based on the\ntiming of the flat wave relative to diurnal heating. As such, \nthunderstorms should weaken and move east, out of the northeast \nmountains, around sunset this evening. The main threat with\nthunderstorms this afternoon and early this evening is damaging\nwind, but hail is also possible.\n\nThe Weather Prediction Center has a Marginal Risk for Excessive\nRainfall for much of the area today given the convective\npotential amid PW values of 1.75 inches. Localized flash \nflooding is possible with stronger cores this afternoon and \nearly this evening. Southern and central portions of the area \nand into the northeast mountains are the most saturated, with 1\nto 3 inches rainfall over the past 3 days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA broad long wave trough drifts eastward across the center of \nthe continent today through Friday, and then eastward to the \ncentral to eastern portion of the continent this weekend. The \nridge rebuilding over the area tonight gives way to the lead \nmid/upper-level short wave trough associated with the long wave \ntrough, which pushes a surface cold front toward the area.\n\nHowever, models are faster with the front, which is now progged\nto cross the Ohio River as early as midday Friday.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has a Slight Risk for Severe \nThunderstorms east of the Ohio river Friday, with a narrow\nMarginal Risk sliver along the Ohio River. Further, most of the\nincrease in bulk shear occurs behind the cold front, so the\nSevere Weather Threat level for Friday is not necessarily much\nhigher compared with today.\n\nWeather Prediction Center has been carrying a Marginal Risk for\nExcessive Rainfall for the forecast area, in a long ribbon all \nalong and ahead of the approaching cold front, for the \nAppalachians and Ohio Valley, and then back westward through the\nsouth-central Plains States.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nWith the mid/upper-level ridge ahead of the flat wave crossing\nthis afternoon, temperatures today climb to around 90 at least\neast of the Ohio River, to go along with the high humidity of \nlate. This pushes heat indices to around 100 F for several\nhours this afternoon, and have issued a Heat Advisory for \ncentral portions of the County Warning Area, which is much of \nthe WV lowlands.\n\nThe faster timing of the cold front on Friday is fast-closing\nthe window of opportunity for excessive heat that afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4... \n\nThe cold front crossing the area Friday, driven by the forward \nprogress of the long wave trough into the eastern U.S., brings \nthe promise of dry weather Saturday, complete with lower dew \npoints, breaking the ad nauseum of active summer weather.\nHowever, high pressure building in behind the cold front as\nearly as overnight Friday night, along with the previous rain, \ncould lead to areas of dense fog early Saturday morning.\n\nThe next cold front brings showers and possible thunderstorms \non Sunday. Models are coming into agreement on the cold front \npushing cleanly through the area as early as Sunday afternoon, \nallowing high pressure to bring dry, cooler and less humid \nweather for the early portion of the upcoming work week, with\nthe next cold front due next Thursday. \n\nProbabilistic-based Central guidance has begun to tease out a\ndry interlude early next week across the middle Ohio Valley, but\nleaves chance PoPs in and near the mountains until Tuesday.",
